# GitHub Actions: validation and explicit deployments

CI runs on pull requests and pushes to develop/main with contents:read only.
It installs locked application dependencies, runs lint, API tests (including contracts),
web tests/build, tracked-secret checks, pip-audit, npm audit and Docker builds.
Cloud projects additionally validate Terraform and run mock-provider plan tests.
Actions are pinned to commit SHAs in .github/action-pins.json. Audit database/network
failures fail the job; findings are not silently ignored. This baseline does not
replace a full secret scanner, integration testing against real providers or review.

Local projects have validation only. Cloud projects also receive DEV and manual PROD
jobs, disabled until repository variables DEV_DEPLOY_ENABLED / PROD_DEPLOY_ENABLED
are set to the exact value true. Credentials are unavailable to PR validation jobs.
Never enable a deployment by switching to pull_request_target or running PR code
on a privileged runner. Protect develop/main and review workflow/script changes.

## Provisioning and image ownership

Provision infrastructure first using the reviewed Terraform instructions in infra/.
The generated infrastructure is DEV only: production infrastructure must be separately
reviewed and provisioned with distinct state, identities, target resources and an
environment=prod label/tag. Never point PROD at DEV. The workflow checks that label
before updating an existing service; it cannot create a missing service.

Terraform owns service configuration; CI/CD owns its image after initial provisioning.
The Terraform service resource ignores image changes so later configuration applies
do not roll back a deployed revision. Change images through this pipeline or an
explicitly reviewed operational procedure. No unattended Terraform apply is generated:
bootstrap, permissions and infrastructure changes retain their own plan/review/apply.

DEV builds the validated commit before authentication, publishes it under its commit
SHA, resolves the registry digest, then deploys by digest. PROD never rebuilds: manual
workflow_dispatch on main requires image_digest (sha256 plus 64 lowercase hex digits)
already published and reviewed in the PROD registry. Copying/promoting an image to
that registry remains a separately authorized step. A push/merge to main alone cannot
trigger production. Both stages serialize deployments and check readiness and health
after verifying that the newly created revision is ready. GCP moves 100% of traffic
to the latest revision; Azure
requires Single revision mode. Canary/multi-revision promotion is outside this path.
Failure does not trigger an automatic rollback; inspect the revision and manually
redeploy a known good digest.

## GitHub Environment configuration

Create separate dev and prod GitHub Environments. Configure prod with required
reviewers, prevent self-review, disallow admin bypass, and a custom deployment branch
policy containing only the branch main. Configure dev to allow develop only.
Merely naming an environment in YAML does not create those protections.

PROD checks the GitHub.com REST API before cloud authentication and fails closed if
protection cannot be verified. Set its environment secret ENVIRONMENT_READ_TOKEN to
a narrowly scoped GitHub App installation token or fine-grained token with repository
administration read access. No write permission is needed. Some GitHub plans/repository
visibilities do not support these protections; PROD remains blocked in that case.
This check targets GitHub.com; GitHub Enterprise Server needs an explicitly reviewed
API-host adaptation. The token is supplied only to the guard step.

Cloud authentication uses OIDC only, with environment-specific federation trust for
repo:OWNER/REPO:environment:dev or :prod. Restrict repository, environment and branch
claims according to provider support. Do not store cloud credential keys in GitHub.
Grant deployment identities only image publishing (DEV), service update/read and the
specific identity/invocation permissions needed for health checks. Use separate PROD
identities. Production registry publication rights are not required for this job.

Environment variables shared by cloud jobs:

- IMAGE_REPOSITORY: full registry/repository path ending in /application, without tag.
- DEPLOY_SERVICE: existing service name. Its environment label/tag must equal dev/prod.

For GCP also configure GCP_PROJECT, GCP_REGION, GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT and
GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER. The service account must be allowed to deploy using
the runtime identity, invoke the service and mint the audience-specific identity
token used for health checks (including the required token-creator impersonation
permission). The service must retain IAM checking and no public IAM members.

For Azure configure AZURE_CLIENT_ID, AZURE_TENANT_ID, AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID,
AZURE_RESOURCE_GROUP and AZURE_REGISTRY_NAME. IMAGE_REPOSITORY must match that ACR.
Azure deployment jobs require a dedicated ephemeral Linux x64 self-hosted runner
labelled harness-azure-internal, with Python 3.12 and Azure CLI installed and network
access FROM THE SAME Container Apps environment. A public GitHub-hosted runner cannot
perform the internal HTTP checks. Azure DEV builds/publishes in a separate hosted job
and passes only its immutable digest to the internal deployment job; that runner
does not need a Docker daemon. Isolate runners by environment and repository;
never share them with untrusted pull-request jobs. The generated runner label needs
an environment-specific runner group policy in GitHub. Missing connectivity makes
health checks fail; the workflow never opens ingress to work around it.

## What is and is not verified

Generation and local workflow validation do not configure GitHub branch protection,
environments, runner groups, OIDC trust or cloud permissions. Those need verification
in the actual repository/account before setting deployment flags. No remote workflow
run, cloud deployment or security-clean status is implied by generating these files.
Generated application tests include their existing optional-provider limitations;
enable real integration tests separately where external systems are required.

